  "slug": "learning-causation-2f3a",
  "summary": "SigDoTS and ProClust add formal significance tests to spatial domains and scRNA-seq groupings, while PRISM and stochprofIC model contamination and noise directly.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is replacing heuristic outputs in single-cell and spatial transcriptomics with explicitly modelled error. SigDoTS applies hypothesis testing with FWER control to spatial domain detection, merging or retaining candidate domains via null simulations. ProClust tests scRNA-seq cluster significance and merges overclustered groups using log-concave modelling and projection-based testing. A related generative-model strand appears in PRISM, an EM framework that jointly models ambient RNA contamination, doublets and cell-type assignment in spatial data. stochprofIC uses distributional regression to correct noise and batch effects when inferring state variability from mini-bulk and split-pool data. Adjacent work addresses calibration elsewhere. Anchor-guided batch correction uses a stable paired omic layer instead of covariates. CellSavior uses confidence-weighted merging for scDNA-seq cell calling. A depth study shows that typical RNA-seq depths miss alternative splicing. SigBridgeR standardizes the benchmarking of phenotype-associated subpopulation methods. What is new is that partitions and corrections now come paired with an explicit null or noise model.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-14"
}
